  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacities of constituents to generate evidence on labour market, foster rural economic growth, and ensure crisis-response through EIIP and Just Transition principles</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A central priority for the ILO in Pakistan is to support a transformative recovery that not only addresses the immediate aftermath of crises but also strengthens the foundations for a more inclusive, sustainable, and resilient future of work. In a country where recurring climate shocks&#x2014;such as the devastating floods of 2022&#x2014;have severely impacted rural livelihoods and exposed deep structural vulnerabilities, there is a critical need to reimagine economic recovery through the lens of equity, environmental sustainability, and social justice. This is particularly urgent in rural areas, where informal employment is widespread, access to opportunities is uneven, and resilience to shocks is limited.

To advance this vision, the ILO is working with national partners to promote strategies that integrate the principles of Just Transition and environmental sustainability into sector policies and rural economic development and crisis response. Employment-Intensive Investment Programme (EIIP) approaches play a pivotal role in this effort, offering communities immediate pathways to income generation while restoring damaged infrastructure, protecting ecosystems, and laying the groundwork for long-term resilience. These interventions are designed not just to rebuild what was lost, but to build better&#x2014;ensuring that investments in recovery and the environment create jobs, support decent work, strengthen social protection systems, contribute to a greener, fairer economy and more resilient communities.
Gender equality is a cross-cutting priority in all these efforts. Women in rural areas, who are disproportionately represented in informal and low-paid work, often face structural barriers to accessing decent employment, training opportunities, and social protection. Climate-related crises further exacerbate these disparities. Therefore, all interventions, including green skills development, EIIP schemes, and labour market programmes, are being designed to promote women&apos;s participati</narrative>
  </description>
